userStories.description:
**Goal** — Give both bot runtimes credential pre-flight and canonical rule-delivery handlers, with schema-owned repair and cost defaults.

**Motivation** — nax#2627: `nax bot` skips the credential pre-flight `nax run` does, so a missing credential surfaces as `callOp[bot-pr-review]: no dispatch completed`.

**Approach** — Bot run setup.
```ts
export async function setupBotRuntime(runtime: NaxRuntime, config: NaxConfig, worktree: string): Promise<void> {
  await runtime.agentManager.validateCredentials();
  await assertDefaultNativeCredentials(config);
  runtime.sessionManager.configureLoopHandlers(
    await configureRunRules({ config, workdir: worktree, pluginHandlers: [] }),
  );
}
```

`reviewCheckout` and `fixCheckout` call it through their deps inside their existing `try`, as the first statement after the bot log opens, so `runtime.close()` in the `finally` still runs when it throws. `workdir` is the PR checkout: the rule handlers read `.nax/rules` from it (base copies for review, which pins base files; head copies for fix). Plugins are not loaded (`pluginHandlers` is empty). A throw propagates; the CLI error boundary prints the message and exits `1`.

`BotConfigSchema` — `src/config/schemas-bot.ts`; `BotConfig` — `src/config/runtime-types-bot.ts`.
- Baseline: schema `{ models }`; interface `{ models: BotModelsConfig }`; `NaxConfig.bot` is optional.
- Target: schema adds `gateRepairRounds: z.number().int().min(0).default(1)` and `costLimit: z.number().positive().default(5)`; the interface adds `gateRepairRounds?: number` and `costLimit?: number`. Readers use `config.bot?.gateRepairRounds ?? DEFAULT_CONFIG.bot?.gateRepairRounds` (and the same for `costLimit`), so the default lives only in the schema.

Add the setupBotRuntime dependency to `_botReviewDeps` and `_botFixDeps`, call it in both runners, export it from `@/bot`, and add both config descriptions in `src/cli/config-descriptions.ts`. New modules import leaf modules only (for example `../execution/lifecycle/run-rules-setup`, never the `@/execution` barrel), as `fix-run.ts` already does for `pre-run-steps`.

Regression migration: `test/helpers/bot-fix.ts` makeBotFixDeps gains `setupBotRuntime: async () => {}`; every other dep is unchanged. `test/helpers/bot-review.ts` makeBotReviewDeps gains the same noop; every other dep is unchanged. `test/integration/bot/review-run.test.ts` adds its own setup noop mock; every existing assertion is unchanged and the file must not grow past 800 lines (baseline 711), so new scenarios go in the new wiring test file. `test/unit/config/bot-config.test.ts` parse({}) now equals `{ models: {}, gateRepairRounds: 1, costLimit: 5 }`, and round-trip parse(bot) includes both defaults; models handling is unchanged.

**Scope** — In: shared setup, runner wiring, config/descriptions, setup tests, fixture migration and verification of the existing CLI error boundary (no new CLI options). Out: repair policy (US-007/008), runtime budget enforcement (US-009), checkout prompts (US-002/003). Plugins are not loaded for bot sessions; only the rule-delivery handlers are configured. Agents other than the native agent ignore the rule-delivery handlers, as they ignore them in `nax run`.

**Interface** — `setupBotRuntime(runtime: NaxRuntime, config: NaxConfig, worktree: string): Promise<void>`; BotConfig adds `gateRepairRounds?: number` and `costLimit?: number`.

**Goal** — Chain one post-push review and ready-only pass inside the fix command's remaining budget.

**Motivation** — nax#2637: after `nax bot fix` pushes, the fixed items stay open until a separate `nax bot review`; the CLI prints only `pushed <sha>`.

**Approach** — Fix cycle.
```ts
export const PR_HEAD_POLL_MS = 5_000;
export const PR_HEAD_WAIT_MS = 60_000;

export async function waitForPrHead(
  input: { ref: PrRef; repoRoot: string; sha: string },
  deps = _fixCycleDeps,          // { readPrInfo, forge, sleep, now }
): Promise<boolean>;

export async function runBotFixCycle(
  input: BotFixInput & { readonly rereview: boolean },   // BotFixInput exported from fix-run.ts
  deps = _fixCycleDeps,          // + { runBotFix, runBotReview, gitToplevel, resolvePrRef }
): Promise<{ fix: BotFixResult; review?: BotReviewResult; ready?: BotFixResult; skipped?: string }>;
```

1. `fix = runBotFix(input)`. Unless `fix.outcome === "pushed"` and `input.rereview`, return `{ fix }`.
2. Resolve `repoRoot` (`gitToplevel(input.dir)`) and `ref` (`resolvePrRef`); `waitForPrHead` polls `readPrInfo` every `PR_HEAD_POLL_MS` until `headSha === fix.pushedSha` or `PR_HEAD_WAIT_MS` has passed (time from the injected `now`, waits through the injected `sleep`). Timeout: return `{ fix, skipped: "PR head did not reach <sha7> within 60 s; run nax bot review <n>" }`.
3. `limit = input.costLimitUsd ?? fix.limitUsd ?? botCostLimit(DEFAULT_CONFIG)`; `remaining = limit - (fix.spentUsd ?? 0)`. When `remaining <= 0`: return `{ fix, skipped: "cost limit reached; run nax bot review <n>" }`.
4. `review = runBotReview({ ...input, costLimitUsd: remaining })`. Not `posted`: return `{ fix, review }`.
5. `ready = runBotFix({ ...input, readyOnly: true, costLimitUsd: remaining - (review.spentUsd ?? 0) })`; return `{ fix, review, ready }`.
6. An error thrown in steps 2-5 is caught and returned as `{ fix, skipped: <message> }` (the push already happened, so its result is never lost).

`readyOnly` in `runBotFix`: runs the PR read, trust, review-of-head refusals and the rulings recording as a fix run does; it skips `runPreRunSteps`, the push-access probe and every saved-fix operation (and ignores `fresh`); the graph starts at `ready` with `{ readyOnly: true, outcome: "ready-pass" }`, and `ready` goes to `done`, so the first run's summary comment is not rewritten.

Add `readyOnly?: boolean` to BotFixInput and BotFixState, plus outcome ready-pass. BOT_FIX_GRAPH ready transitions: `{ to: "done", when: readyOnly === true }` then `{ to: "report" }`. Reuse existing readyStep CI/rulings verdict rather than creating another policy. Export runBotFixCycle and waitForPrHead through @/bot, using leaf imports internally. Inject time/sleep and forge dependencies; no real polling waits in tests.

**Scope** — In: polling, bounded chaining, remaining budget, post-push error preservation and ready-only entry/exemptions. Out: CLI flags/output (US-013), saved-fix policies (US-010/011), base cost guard (US-009). The fix cycle runs at most one re-review and one ready pass; it never runs a second fix session.

**Interface** — TypeScript signatures/constants above; result `{ fix: BotFixResult; review?: BotReviewResult; ready?: BotFixResult; skipped?: string }`.
